WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.795 --> 00:00:08.540
In this unit, we'll talk about
how the assembler represents and

2
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.221
handles symbols using binary code.

3
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.315
So the general problem that we
are facing is writing an assembler for

4
00:00:16.315 --> 00:00:18.128
the Hack machine language.

5
00:00:18.128 --> 00:00:23.013
And the development strategy
that we adopted was one

6
00:00:23.013 --> 00:00:28.130
of delaying the treatment of
symbols to a later stage.

7
00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:32.030
Well, this later stage has has come up,
and

8
00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:36.860
we now have to deal with
symbols explicitly.

9
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:41.290
I want to remind you that we already
know how to handle white space and

10
00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:46.570
instructions, and
therefore we now want to focus on symbols.

11
00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:47.440
And as usual,

12
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.830
I use red ink to highlight all
the symbols in this particular example.

13
00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:56.650
And as you can see,
there are quite a few of them.

14
00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:02.579
And I think I mentioned it before, this is
very typical in programming in general and

15
00:01:02.579 --> 00:01:06.082
machine language
programming in particular.

16
00:01:06.082 --> 00:01:09.620
The more symbols you have,
the more expressive and

17
00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:13.401
easy to read is the program,
and therefore, we,

18
00:01:13.401 --> 00:01:18.501
we encourage programmers to use symbols,
which means that the assembler

19
00:01:18.501 --> 00:01:24.400
has to work hard to resolve these
symbols into, into binary code.

20
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.710
But once we do it, we achieve something
very remarkable, because symbolic

21
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:36.190
programming is a very nice abstraction
that improves the quality of your program,

22
00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:40.120
and the quality of life of
the programmer significantly.

23
00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:45.830
Okay, so, what kind of symbols
do we have in Hack programs?

24
00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:50.790
As it turns out,
they fall into three distinct categories.

25
00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.670
First of all, we have variable symbols.

26
00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:58.150
Variable symbols represent memory
locations where the programmer wants to

27
00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:04.450
maintain some values that typically change
in the course of executing the program.

28
00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:08.550
Now, this sentence is a little bit
misleading, because the programmer

29
00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:12.990
doesn't care at all where these variables
are actually located in memory.

30
00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:17.610
You know, as far as the programmer is,
cons, is concerned he or

31
00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:22.232
she is going to say something like @sum,
or @x,

32
00:02:22.232 --> 00:02:26.930
or @y, and let the assembler worry about

33
00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:32.220
where to store these or
represent these variables in memory.

34
00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.650
Once again,
this is a very nice abstraction

35
00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:39.040
that the assembler delivers
to the practicing programmer.

36
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.769
So this is one kind of of
symbols we have to worry about.

37
00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.240
Another kind of symbols is
label symbols that represent

38
00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:52.480
destinations of goto command,
commands in plural.

39
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.260
And in this example that we
are facing here, we have three such

40
00:02:57.260 --> 00:03:02.670
symbols LOOP, STOP, and END.

41
00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:07.460
And finally, we may have all
sorts of predefined symbols

42
00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:12.330
like virtual registers, screen,
keyboard, and so on and so forth.

43
00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.520
And in the example that we have here,
I think we have two,

44
00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:22.340
we happen to have two predefined symbols,
which are R0 and R1.

45
00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.830
So, if you want to deal with symbols,

46
00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:29.030
these are the three kinds of
symbols that you have to handle.

47
00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:33.940
So let me begin to describe what to do

48
00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:36.530
with every one of these symbols,
and we'll start from the end.

49
00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:40.250
We'll start with predefined symbols.

50
00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:45.010
So according to the Hack
language specification,

51
00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:49.290
the language features
23 pre-defined symbols.

52
00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.950
And here you see all of them.

53
00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:58.160
And how do you translate such
a symbol into binary code?

54
00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.600
Well, first of all, we have to realize
that these symbols come to play only

55
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.310
in the context of a instructions.

56
00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:08.700
So the only play,
place where you can see such a symbol

57
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:13.890
popping up in a program is
an instruction like @preDefinedSymbol.

58
00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:17.210
How do you translate this
instruction into binary?

59
00:04:17.210 --> 00:04:22.050
Well, you simply replace the predefined
symbols with its corresponding value,

60
00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:23.830
which is a decimal number.

61
00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:27.920
And now the only thing that remains
is to translate the at decimal number

62
00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:31.770
into binary, and this is something that
we discussed in the previous unit.

63
00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:36.210
So, case closed,
we know how to handle predefined values.

64
00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.830
The next category of symbols
that we have to deal with

65
00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:43.070
are symbols that denote labels.

66
00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:48.970
Now, label symbols are used
to denote destinations in

67
00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:54.380
the program that I may want to
jump to using goto commands.

68
00:04:54.380 --> 00:05:00.160
And they are declared very
specifically using the pseudo-command,

69
00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.960
round parentheses, and
the name of the label in the middle.

70
00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:12.340
I've used XXX to, to stand for this label,
which can be any sequence of characters.

71
00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:16.740
And these things are called
pseudo-commands because

72
00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:19.150
they don't generate any code.

73
00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:23.950
When we translate the program into binary,
we don't translate

74
00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:27.890
the label declaration instructions, and
that's why they're called pseudo-commands.

75
00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:31.980
And we'll,
we'll talk about this further later on.

76
00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:37.410
So we declare label symbols using
the agreed upon parentheses.

77
00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.860
And you might ask yourself,
why parentheses?

78
00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.040
Why not label colon or
something like this?

79
00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.080
Well when Norm and I designed this
language, we decided to use this

80
00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:53.378
syntax once again, for
reasons that will become clear later on.

81
00:05:53.378 --> 00:05:58.916
Now once we declare a symbol
using round parentheses,

82
00:05:58.916 --> 00:06:04.231
the meaning of this declaration
is that from now on,

83
00:06:04.231 --> 00:06:07.553
whenever we see XXX in the program,

84
00:06:07.553 --> 00:06:13.091
we mean to replace it with
the address of the memory location

85
00:06:13.091 --> 00:06:18.004
that contains the next
instruction in the program.

86
00:06:18.004 --> 00:06:20.989
Now, in order to make
sense of what I just said,

87
00:06:20.989 --> 00:06:23.836
I have to keep track of
instruction numbers.

88
00:06:23.836 --> 00:06:28.322
And that's what I do next, if you look
at the left hand side of the slide,

89
00:06:28.322 --> 00:06:33.178
you will see that I have marked or labeled
every instruction in the program with

90
00:06:33.178 --> 00:06:36.480
a running number that starts with zero.

91
00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:42.605
And notice that I'm skipping empty lines,
empty lines are white space, which is

92
00:06:43.667 --> 00:06:48.836
tossed away, and also,
I'm skipping the pseudo-instructions.

93
00:06:48.836 --> 00:06:53.168
So, label declaration
instructions like LOOP, STOP,

94
00:06:53.168 --> 00:06:57.500
and END I've not counted, so to speak.

95
00:06:57.500 --> 00:07:02.097
Now, once I have these line
numbers in front of me, or

96
00:07:02.097 --> 00:07:05.775
once I remember it somewhere in my memory,

97
00:07:05.775 --> 00:07:09.859
now I see that I can relate
LOOP to the number 4,

98
00:07:09.859 --> 00:07:14.378
STOP to the number 18,
and END to the number 22.

99
00:07:14.378 --> 00:07:16.948
So I can basically generate
this association and

100
00:07:16.948 --> 00:07:19.170
keep it in the back of my mind.

101
00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:24.180
So from now on,
whenever I see an instruction like @LOOP,

102
00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.510
we actually mean @4.

103
00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:30.860
You know, we want to go to instruction
number four, and so on and so forth for

104
00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:35.330
the, for all the other label
symbols in your program.

105
00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:40.250
So how do you deal with
an @labelSymbol instruction?

106
00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:44.796
Well, all you have to do is look
up the value of this instruction,

107
00:07:44.796 --> 00:07:48.763
which you figured out before,
as, as I just explained,

108
00:07:48.763 --> 00:07:51.923
and replace it with the the symbol.

109
00:07:51.923 --> 00:07:57.378
What remains is an @value instruction
where value is a decimal number.

110
00:07:57.378 --> 00:08:00.190
And, we know how to deal with this,

111
00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:03.980
because we discussed it
in the previous unit.

112
00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:08.810
So, that's how you deal with
symbols that represent labels.

113
00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:11.960
The last category of symbols
that we have to deal with

114
00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:15.160
are symbols that represent variables.

115
00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:19.029
Now, as you recall, when you wrote
programs in the hex-symbolic language,

116
00:08:19.029 --> 00:08:19.622
language.

117
00:08:19.622 --> 00:08:23.345
We have this,
fantastic ability to create and

118
00:08:23.345 --> 00:08:27.070
use as many symbolic variables as we want.

119
00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:30.870
This is one of the most important
abstractions in programming.

120
00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:33.740
And, someone has to pay the price,
so to speak.

121
00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:36.705
Someone has to implement this abstraction.

122
00:08:36.705 --> 00:08:38.750
And as, as you can imagine,

123
00:08:38.750 --> 00:08:43.270
the agent that implements this
obstruction is the assembler.

124
00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:44.490
How do we do it?

125
00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:49.550
Well according to the Hack language
specification, any symbol that appears

126
00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:54.550
in the program which is not
pre-defined and is not accompanied by

127
00:08:54.550 --> 00:09:00.800
another label declaration statement
is considered a variable.

128
00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:04.190
We have two such variables in
the example in front of us, and

129
00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:07.290
they're called i and sum.

130
00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:12.154
And the program, as you can see, begins
with four lines of code that basically

131
00:09:12.154 --> 00:09:18.140
declare and initialize these two, these
two variables to 1 and 0, respectively.

132
00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:19.450
It's not terribly interesting, but

133
00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:23.912
I'm just trying to add some
color to this discussion.

134
00:09:23.912 --> 00:09:28.535
All right, so we know,
how the programmer expresses variables,

135
00:09:28.535 --> 00:09:30.705
in the way that I just described.

136
00:09:30.705 --> 00:09:34.020
How do we handle these variables
if we are the assembler?

137
00:09:34.020 --> 00:09:38.766
Well, each such variable
is assigned a unique

138
00:09:38.766 --> 00:09:42.788
memory address, starting with 16.

139
00:09:42.788 --> 00:09:45.020
You may ask yourself, why 16?

140
00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:50.060
Well, this is a decision that Norm and
I made when we,

141
00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:54.310
developed this, language,
and this, assembler.

142
00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:58.505
And it's not exactly arbitrary decision,

143
00:09:58.505 --> 00:10:02.450
as you will see later on, but for now you
can treat it as an arbitrary decision.

144
00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:07.871
So variables are assigned to
memory from address 16 onward.

145
00:10:07.871 --> 00:10:08.891
All right, so

146
00:10:08.891 --> 00:10:13.996
with that in mind we have only two
variables in this program, i and sum.

147
00:10:13.996 --> 00:10:19.530
And the values of these variables
are going to be 16 and 17.

148
00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:24.420
Now, like any other symbol in
a Hack program, the only context in

149
00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:29.720
which such symbol can come to play is
in the context of an A instruction.

150
00:10:29.720 --> 00:10:35.081
So we, we may, we may see
instructions like @variable name.

151
00:10:35.081 --> 00:10:36.704
We definitely we see them,

152
00:10:36.704 --> 00:10:41.110
will see them because otherwise why did
we declare these variables to begin with.

153
00:10:41.110 --> 00:10:42.950
We want to act on these variables and

154
00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:48.240
we do it using A instructions
followed by C instructions.

155
00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.750
So, whenever we see such, such
an instruction, how do we translate it?

156
00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:55.630
Well, all you have to do is the following.

157
00:10:55.630 --> 00:10:58.590
If you see this instruction for
the first time in the program,

158
00:10:58.590 --> 00:11:02.030
if this variable appears in
the program for the first time,

159
00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:08.320
then you allocate it to a memory address,
starting from address 16 onward.

160
00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:12.520
If you see this value popping
up later in the program,

161
00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:17.240
you simply look up the value that
you assigned to it before, and

162
00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:22.520
then what remains is
an @decimal value instruction,

163
00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:25.510
which we already know how to handle.

164
00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:29.090
So this is how we handle symbols
that represent variables.

165
00:11:30.490 --> 00:11:34.730
Now, I think that you
will agree with me that

166
00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:38.760
handling all these different kinds
of symbols is a major headache.

167
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.270
And what we can we possibly
do to make this task simpler?

168
00:11:45.270 --> 00:11:47.310
Well, exactly for this reason,

169
00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:52.900
computer scientists have invented
an artifact called symbol table.

170
00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:55.300
The symbol table is a very simple and

171
00:11:55.300 --> 00:12:00.191
powerful data structure that
enables you to store and

172
00:12:00.191 --> 00:12:04.440
use symbol value pairs, and

173
00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:10.880
I can populate the table with as
many symbol value pairs as I please.

174
00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:17.300
So, when I write an assembler,
I construct such an empty symbol table,

175
00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:22.460
and then I begin to populate it with
all the symbols that I encounter in,

176
00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:25.220
in the program that I am
supposed to translate.

177
00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:30.105
And that's also how I'm going to
explain to to you the structure and

178
00:12:30.105 --> 00:12:32.039
the use of the symbol table.

179
00:12:32.039 --> 00:12:34.288
I'm going to do it constructively.

180
00:12:34.288 --> 00:12:39.104
I'm going to describe how we actually
build the symbol table in order to deal

181
00:12:39.104 --> 00:12:41.460
with the example that we see here.

182
00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:44.050
And by the way, the same will hold for

183
00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:48.650
any other Hack program that you will,
that you will see in the future.

184
00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:50.770
So how do you create this symbol table?

185
00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:55.746
Well, the first thing that you do is you,
you construct an empty symbol table.

186
00:12:55.746 --> 00:12:59.944
And then you populate it with
all the predefined symbols which

187
00:12:59.944 --> 00:13:02.590
are specified in the language.

188
00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:07.570
So in the case of the Hack assembly
language we have 23 such pairs.

189
00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:12.110
Simply add them up to
the table one by one.

190
00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:16.590
And you do this, by the way, before
you even touch, the source program and

191
00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:18.790
before you start any translation.

192
00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:21.170
That's how you initialize
the symbol table.

193
00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:23.580
Now what do you do next?

194
00:13:23.580 --> 00:13:30.200
The next thing that you do is
you march through the entire

195
00:13:30.200 --> 00:13:35.860
text file that constitutes
your source assembly program.

196
00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:41.720
And the only thing that you do is
you look for label declarations.

197
00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:48.000
Look for lines of code that
begins with left parenthesis.

198
00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:52.340
Once you have such line of code, you
know that if the code was well written,

199
00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:57.530
if it contains no error, it must be
the beginning of a label declaration.

200
00:13:57.530 --> 00:14:04.160
Now as you do this, you also keep track
of how many lines you read so far.

201
00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:09.040
And, as I explained previously,
you count only real instructions,

202
00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:12.060
skipping label declarations and
white space.

203
00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:19.401
So, once you do this, if you have this
count in mind, when you encounter LOOP,

204
00:14:19.401 --> 00:14:24.330
for example, you should know
that LOOP corresponds to four.

205
00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:25.299
And then you go on,

206
00:14:25.299 --> 00:14:30.310
you ignore everything else until you hit
the next label declaration which is STOP.

207
00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.660
And then you consult your counter,

208
00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:37.130
you see that stop corresponds to 18 and
so on and, and so forth.

209
00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:44.663
Based on this scanning, you can
continue to build the symbol table.

210
00:14:44.663 --> 00:14:50.231
And at the end of this process, you have
added to the symbol table, all the symbols

211
00:14:50.231 --> 00:14:55.680
that represent go to destinations
in your program, label symbols.

212
00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:00.350
Now, we call this process, first pass.

213
00:15:00.350 --> 00:15:03.260
So, the assembler that
we are going to develop,

214
00:15:03.260 --> 00:15:07.580
is going to be a two
pass assembly process.

215
00:15:07.580 --> 00:15:12.330
In the first pass,
we extract from the program

216
00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:17.030
all the label symbols, and in the second
pass, which I'm going to discuss next,

217
00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:20.010
we're going to extract
all the variable symbols.

218
00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:23.440
So here's what we do in the next pass.

219
00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:28.660
Once we finish the first pass,
we start once again,

220
00:15:28.660 --> 00:15:32.810
to scan the entire program
from beginning to end.

221
00:15:32.810 --> 00:15:38.482
And whenever we see a label, or
I'm sorry, whenever we see a symbol

222
00:15:38.482 --> 00:15:44.670
which does not appear in the symbol table,
we know that it's a variable.

223
00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:49.513
So, we add it to the table, and
we assign to it the values 16, 17,

224
00:15:49.513 --> 00:15:54.628
18, 19, and so on, for as many
variables as you have in the program.

225
00:15:54.628 --> 00:15:58.992
And when we encounter these
variables later on in the program,

226
00:15:58.992 --> 00:16:01.970
we always look them up
in the symbol table.

227
00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:06.207
If we find them in the symbol table,
well, they are declared already and

228
00:16:06.207 --> 00:16:07.186
we can use them.

229
00:16:07.186 --> 00:16:11.290
If we don't find them, we can
conclude that it's a new variable and

230
00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:13.525
